{# =====================================================================
   Template: node_plan_global_consult  (Module M05 — craft-consultant second pass)
   Rendered by prompts/prompt_loader.py (StrictUndefined, autoescape=False).

   A single-shot craft critique of an ALREADY-VALIDATED global plan. The consultant
   proposes a tightened, less templated revision; the harness re-runs the authoritative
   validators and only keeps the revision if it still passes. The consultant never grades
   its own work in.

   Expected context variables (ALL required — StrictUndefined):
     plan         : str  — the current validated global plan, as a JSON string.
     genre        : str  — book genre / category.
     premise_seed : str  — the user's brief / creative seed.
   ===================================================================== #}
<craft_consultant_task>

  <role>
    You are a seasoned story-structure consultant. You are handed a global plan that already
    passes the structural checks but may be generic or templated. Your job is to make it
    sharper and more distinctive WITHOUT breaking its structure — tighten the central
    conflict, differentiate the arcs so each does a genuinely different job, and make each
    promise/payoff pairing specific and non-circular.
  </role>

  <context>
    <genre>{{ genre }}</genre>
    <premise_seed>{{ premise_seed | xmlsafe }}</premise_seed>
    <current_plan>{{ plan | xmlsafe }}</current_plan>
  </context>

  <instructions>
    Return a revised global plan that keeps the SAME keys and structure as the current plan:
    premise, central_conflict, ending_target, arcs (each with arc_id, title, function,
    word_allocation), and promises (each with id, promise, payoff). Preserve every arc_id and
    promise id from the current plan so the revision maps cleanly onto it. Improve the
    content: every arc's "function" must be distinct (no two arcs doing the same job) and each
    promise's "payoff" must say something concretely different from its "promise". Do not add
    prose, scenes, or beats — this is still structure, not draft.
  </instructions>

  <output_contract>
    Return EXACTLY ONE JSON object with this shape and no other keys, and nothing else
    (no prose, no markdown fence):
    {% raw %}
    {
      "premise": "<text>",
      "central_conflict": "<text>",
      "ending_target": "<text>",
      "arcs": [ { "arc_id": "<id>", "title": "<text>", "function": "<distinct role>", "word_allocation": <int> } ],
      "promises": [ { "id": "<id>", "promise": "<text>", "payoff": "<concretely different from promise>" } ]
    }
    {% endraw %}
  </output_contract>

</craft_consultant_task>
